The correct answer is (c) ZnO.  **Explanation**:  - The reduction of a metal oxide to the metal by carbon occurs when the standard reduction potential of the metal is higher than that of carbon.  - The reduction potential of zinc (Zn) is higher than that of carbon, but the bond between zinc and oxygen in ZnO is very strong due to the small radius of Zn²⁺ ion. This strong bond requires higher temperatures and other stronger reducing agents than carbon can provide.  - Al₂O₃, PbO, and Fe₂O₃ can be reduced by carbon. For example:  \[
\text{Al}_2\text{O}_3 + 3\text{C} \rightarrow 2\text{Al} + 3\text{CO}
\]  \[
\text{PbO} + \text{C} \rightarrow \text{Pb} + \text{CO}
\]  \[
\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3 + 3\text{C} \rightarrow 2\text{Fe} + 3\text{CO}
\]  Thus, ZnO is the only one that lacks the ease of reduction by carbon.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

