1. **(\(a \equiv b \pmod{10} \implies a \equiv b \pmod{2} \text{ and } a \equiv b \pmod{5}\))**
   Suppose \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\). By the definition of conjugacy, this means \(10 \mid (a - b)\), so \(a - b = 10k\) for some integer \(k\).

   Since \(10 = 2 \cdot 5\), it implies \(2 \mid (a - b)\) and \(5 \mid (a - b)\). By the definition of conjugacy, this implies \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \equiv \text{mod} \quad 5\).

2. **(\(a \equiv b \pmod{2} \text{ and } a \equiv b \pmod{5} \implies a \equiv b \pmod{10}\))**
   Suppose \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\). This means \(2 \mid (a - b)\) and \(5 \mid (a - b)\). Thus, \(a - b = 2k\) and \(a - b = 5l\) for integers \(k\) and \(l\).

   Since \(2 \mid (a - b)\) and \(5 \mid (a - b)\), and \(2\) and \(5\) are realize, it implies \(10 \mid (a - b)\). Thus, \(a - b = 10m\) for some integer \(m\). By the definition of conjugacy, this implies \(a \equiv b \leq \text{mod} \quad 10\).

