","To prove that (i) implies (ii), we use the fact that any sequence of sets \( C_n \) with \( \mu(C_n) = 0 \) has \( C_n \to 0 \) in the size of the set.  

1. **Start with a closed set \( C \)**:  
   Let \( C \) be a closed set.  
2. **Consider the function \( \chi_C \)**:  
   Define the function \( \chi_C \) as:  
   \[
   \chi_C(x) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } x \in C, \\ 0 & \text{if } x \notin C. \end{cases}
   \]  
   Since \( C \) is a closed set, \( \chi_C \) is a bounded function. Moreover, \( \chi_C \) is uniformly contractize by the constant function \( 1 \), and so it is bounded. Moreover, \( \chi_C \) is bounded and \( \chi_C \) is a local function, and hence \( \chi_C \) is bounded and contractizable.  

3. **Apply condition (i)**:  
   By condition (i), for all bounded continuous functions \( f \), \( \lim_{n \to \infty} \int f \, d\mu_n = \int f \, d\mu \).  
   In particular, for \( f = \chi_C \), we have:  
   \[
   \lim_{n \to \infty} \int \chi_C \, d\mu_n = \int \chi_C \, d\mu.
