","To determine the strong coupling constant, αs, in particle physics experiments, several methods are used. The most precise method is based on the precision precision measurements of the anomalous magnetic moment of the muon (g-μ) and comparing the result with theoretical predictions that involve αs.

The strong coupling constant (αs) is a fundamental parameter in quantum chromodynamics (QCD), the theory of the strong nuclear force. It determines the strength of the strong interaction between quarks and gluons. The value of αs is not fixed but depends on the energy scale at which it is measured. This energy dependence is described by the renormalization group equation, which is a result of the asymptotic freedom of QCD.

