","(a) The force on the proton is given by \(F = qE\), where \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\). The magnitude of the force is:
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]
Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), we can find the acceleration:
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]

(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial velocity is \(v_0 = 0\). The final velocity is \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\). Using the equation \(v = v_0 + at\), we solve for \(t\):
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0}{4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.32 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{s}
\]

Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\) and the time taken to reach \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is \(7.32 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{s}\).

