","The maximum deflection of a triangular-wall cantilever beam with a concentrated load at the end is given by:

\[
\delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{P L^3}{3 E I}
\]

where \( I \) is the moment of inertia of the beam's cross-section. For a triangular cross-section, the moment of inertia about the axis of bending is:

\[
I = \frac{b t^3}{12}
\]

Substituting \( I \) into the deflection equation:

\[
\delta_{\text{max}} = \frac{P L^3}{3 E \left( \frac{b t^3}{12} \right)} = \frac{P L^3}{3 E b t^3} \times 12 = \frac{4 P L^3}{E b t^3}
\]

