\[
\kappa_1 = \frac{M}{H}
\]
where \( M \) is the magnetic susceptibility and \( H \) is the applied magnetic field.

The effect of a second material with its coaxial magnetic susceptibility \( \kappa' \) on the induced magnetization coefficient \( \kappa_1 \) of the first material depends on the geometry of the system and the relative permeability of the materials. The magnetic field \( H \) is not the same as the applied magnetic field \( H_0 \). Instead, \( H \) is the total magnetic field inside the first material.

