3 = Δt / √(1 - (0.6c)²/c²)

3 = Δt / √(1 - 0.36)

3 = Δt / √(0.64)

3 = Δt / 0.8

Δt = 3 * 0.8

Δt = 2.4 years

Now, we need to find the time experienced by the clock in the cargo body relative to Earth. Since the clocks are on the same spaceship, they will experience the same time dilation. Therefore, the clock in the cargo body will also have been moving for 3 years as measured by the spaceship's perspective. However, we want to find the time on the cargo clock as measured by an observer on Earth. Since the clock in the cargo body is on the spaceship, it will also experience the same time dilation as the clock in the cockpit. Therefore, the time on the cargo clock relative to Earth is the same as the time on the cockpit clock relative to Earth, which is 2.4 years.

