","To calculate the work done by the gas during the isothermal expansion, we can use the formula for work done in an isothermal process:

\[
W = -nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right)
\]

where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( n = \text{number of moles} \)
- \( R = 8.314 \text{ J/mol·K} = 0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K} \)
- \( T = \text{temperature} \)
- \( V_1 = \text{initial volume} \)
- \( V_2 = \text{final volume} \)

Given:
- \( n = 2 \text{ moles} \)
- \( P_1 = 2 \text{ atm} \)
- \( V_1 = 4 \text{ L} \)
- \( V_2 = 8 \text{ L} \)

Since the process is isothermal, the temperature \( T \) is constant. We can use the ideal gas law to find the temperature:

\[
PV = nRT
\]

\[
T = \frac{PV}{nR}
\]

Using \( P_1 = 2 \text{ atm} \), \( V_1 = 4 \text{ L} \), \( n = 2 \text{ moles} \), and \( R = 0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K} \):

\[
T = \frac{(2 \text{ atm})(4 \text{ L})}{(2 \text{ mol})(0.0821 \text{ L·atm/mol·K})} = \frac{8}{0.1642} \text{ K} \approx 48.8 \text{ K}
\]

Now, we can calculate the work done:

\[
W = -nRT \ln \left( \frac{V_2}{V_1} \right) = -2 \times 0.0821 \times 48.8 \ln \left( \frac{8}{4} \right)
\]

\[
W = -2 \times 0.0821 \times 48.8 \ln(2) = -0.81 \times 48.8 \times 0.693 \approx -2.75 \times 0.693 \text{ L·atm}
\]

\[
W \approx -2.75 \text{ L·atm}
\]

Converting to joules:
\[
1 \text{ L·atm} = 101.325 \text{ J}
\]

\[
W = -2.75 \times 101.325 \text{ J} \approx -278.6 \text{ J}
\]

Thus, the work done by the gas during the expansion process is \(\boxed{-278.6 \text{ J}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
